Definition The anecdotal fallacy uses a personal experience or isolated example as though it were sufficient evidence for a broader conclusion.
Example &ldquo;My grandfather smoked every day and lived to be 95, so smoking can&rsquo;t be that dangerous.&rdquo;
One person&rsquo;s experience does not outweigh broader evidence about health risks.
Why It&rsquo;s Fallacious Individual experiences can be informative, but they may not be representative of the larger population.
How to Recognize It Ask:
&ldquo;Is this single example representative of the broader claim?&rdquo;

Definition An appeal to expertise occurs when an expert&rsquo;s opinion is treated as sufficient proof without adequately considering the expert&rsquo;s relevant qualifications, evidence, or the degree of agreement among qualified experts.
Example &ldquo;A physicist says this historical claim is true, so it must be true.&rdquo;
Expertise in physics does not automatically establish expertise in history.
Why It&rsquo;s Fallacious Expert testimony can be valuable evidence. The problem occurs when expertise is treated as infallible or when an expert&rsquo;s credentials are unrelated to the claim.
How to Recognize It Ask:
&ldquo;Is this person actually an appropriate expert on this particular claim?&rdquo;

Definition An appeal to fear attempts to establish a conclusion by emphasizing frightening consequences rather than providing sufficient evidence for the conclusion.
Example &ldquo;If you don&rsquo;t support this surveillance program, criminals will take over the city.&rdquo;
The frightening prediction does not establish that the surveillance program is necessary or effective.
Why It&rsquo;s Fallacious Fear can be a legitimate response to genuine danger, but the emotional reaction does not substitute for evidence that the proposed conclusion is justified.
How to Recognize It Ask:
&ldquo;What evidence supports the conclusion apart from the threat being described?&rdquo;

Definition An appeal to pity attempts to establish a conclusion by provoking sympathy for someone rather than providing relevant evidence for the conclusion.
Example &ldquo;You should give me a passing grade because I&rsquo;ve had a really difficult semester.&rdquo;
The student&rsquo;s circumstances may deserve compassion, but they do not by themselves establish that the student has met the requirements for passing.
Why It&rsquo;s Fallacious Sympathy can be morally relevant to some decisions, but feeling sorry for someone does not establish that a factual claim is true or that a particular conclusion follows.
How to Recognize It Ask:
&ldquo;Is sympathy being used instead of evidence relevant to the conclusion?&rdquo;

Definition An appeal to probability occurs when an argument treats something that is likely or possible as though it must be true.
Example &ldquo;There&rsquo;s a 90% chance the package will arrive today, so it will definitely arrive today.&rdquo;
A high probability is not certainty.
Why It&rsquo;s Fallacious Probability describes uncertainty. A probable outcome can still fail to occur.
How to Recognize It Look for a shift from:
&ldquo;probably&rdquo;
or
&ldquo;likely&rdquo;
to:
&ldquo;therefore it must be true.&rdquo;

Definition An appeal to tradition argues that a belief or practice is correct or preferable simply because it has existed for a long time.
Example &ldquo;We&rsquo;ve always held the meeting this way, so changing the process would be wrong.&rdquo;
The age of a practice does not establish that it is effective.
Why It&rsquo;s Fallacious Tradition can provide useful historical information, but it does not by itself establish that something is correct or optimal.
How to Recognize It Ask:
&ldquo;What evidence supports the practice apart from the fact that it is traditional?&rdquo;

Definition The bandwagon fallacy argues that a claim is true, correct, or worthwhile because many people believe it or do it.
Example &ldquo;Millions of people use this product, so it must be the best.&rdquo;
Popularity does not establish quality.
Why It&rsquo;s Fallacious Large numbers of people can be mistaken. The truth or quality of a claim requires relevant evidence rather than popularity alone.
How to Recognize It Ask:
&ldquo;Is popularity being offered as evidence of truth or correctness?&rdquo;

Definition The burden of proof principle holds that someone making a claim is generally responsible for providing sufficient evidence to support that claim.
Example &ldquo;I claim that an invisible dragon lives in my garage. You can&rsquo;t prove that it doesn&rsquo;t, so my claim stands.&rdquo;
The person making the extraordinary claim cannot establish it merely by demanding that others disprove it.
Why It&rsquo;s Fallacious A claim does not become established simply because nobody has disproven it.
How to Recognize It Ask:
&ldquo;Who is making the claim, and what evidence have they provided for it?&rdquo;

Definition Cherry picking occurs when someone selectively presents evidence favorable to their conclusion while ignoring relevant evidence that points in another direction.
Example &ldquo;Here are five people who succeeded without attending college. Therefore, college isn&rsquo;t necessary for success.&rdquo;
The argument ignores the many other relevant cases that might contradict the conclusion.
Why It&rsquo;s Fallacious Evidence must be evaluated as a whole rather than selectively choosing only the observations that support a preferred conclusion.
How to Recognize It Ask:
&ldquo;What relevant evidence has been left out?&rdquo;

Definition The fallacy of composition occurs when a property of individual parts is assumed to necessarily apply to the whole.
Example &ldquo;Every player on this team is an excellent athlete, so the team must be the best team in the league.&rdquo;
The quality of individual players does not necessarily determine the quality of the team as a whole.
Why It&rsquo;s Fallacious Properties of individual components do not always transfer to the system or group they compose.
How to Recognize It Ask:
&ldquo;Does this property necessarily transfer from each part to the whole?&rdquo;

Definition A domino effect argument claims that one event will inevitably trigger a sequence of additional events, usually ending in a significant consequence.
Example &ldquo;If we allow one exception to this rule, everyone will demand an exception and the entire system will collapse.&rdquo;
The argument does not establish that each step will actually follow from the previous one.
Why It&rsquo;s Fallacious A causal chain needs evidence at each important link. The mere possibility of a sequence does not make the sequence inevitable.
How to Recognize It Ask:
&ldquo;What evidence supports each step in the proposed chain?&rdquo;

Definition Equivocation occurs when an argument uses a key word or phrase in more than one sense while treating those different meanings as though they were the same.
Example &ldquo;A feather is light. What is light cannot be dark. Therefore, a feather cannot be dark.&rdquo;
The word &ldquo;light&rdquo; changes meaning between &ldquo;not heavy&rdquo; and &ldquo;bright.&rdquo;
Why It&rsquo;s Fallacious The argument appears to connect its premises through the repeated word, but the word&rsquo;s meaning changes along the way.
How to Recognize It Identify important words that appear multiple times and ask:
&ldquo;Does this word mean exactly the same thing every time it is used?&rdquo;

Visualize │ ▼ B HAPPENS │ ▼ ┌─────────────┐ │ A CAUSED B │ └─────────────┘ ✕ CAUSATION NOT SHOWN Definition A false cause fallacy occurs when an argument incorrectly identifies one thing as the cause of another.
Example &ldquo;The city installed new streetlights, and crime went down. Therefore, the streetlights caused the reduction in crime.&rdquo;
Other factors could have contributed to the change.
Why It&rsquo;s Fallacious An observed association or sequence does not necessarily establish a causal relationship.
How to Recognize It Ask:
&ldquo;What alternative causes could explain this outcome?&rdquo;

Visualize PROBLEM │ ▼ ┌───────────┐ │ TWO │ │ OPTIONS │ └───────────┘ ╱ ╲ ▼ ▼ OPTION A OPTION B ╲ ╱ ╲ ╱ ✕ OTHER OPTIONS IGNORED Definition A false dilemma presents a situation as having only two possible choices when additional alternatives exist.
Example &ldquo;Either you support this proposal or you don&rsquo;t care about the community.&rdquo;
Someone could care about the community while disagreeing with the proposal.
Why It&rsquo;s Fallacious The argument artificially limits the available options. Rejecting one presented option does not necessarily mean accepting the other.
How to Recognize It Look for language such as:
&ldquo;Either A or B.&rdquo;
Then ask:
&ldquo;Are A and B really the only possibilities?&rdquo;

Definition The genetic fallacy evaluates a claim based on where it came from rather than on the evidence supporting the claim.
Example &ldquo;That idea came from a conspiracy theorist, so it must be false.&rdquo;
The origin of an idea does not by itself determine whether the idea is true.
Why It&rsquo;s Fallacious A claim can come from an unreliable source and still happen to be true. Conversely, a claim from a reputable source can still be false.
How to Recognize It Ask:
&ldquo;Am I evaluating the claim itself, or only where it came from?&rdquo;

Definition The hot-hand fallacy is the belief that someone who has recently succeeded is more likely to succeed again because they are &ldquo;on a streak,&rdquo; even when the outcomes are independent.
Example &ldquo;She&rsquo;s made five three-point shots in a row, so she is definitely more likely to make the next one.&rdquo;
A streak alone does not necessarily change the underlying probability of the next shot.
Why It&rsquo;s Fallacious Past success does not automatically increase the probability of future success when outcomes are independent.
How to Recognize It Ask:
&ldquo;Does the previous streak actually affect the probability of the next outcome?&rdquo;

Definition A loaded question is phrased in a way that assumes something that has not been established, making the respondent appear to accept that assumption by answering the question.
Example &ldquo;Have you stopped cheating on your exams?&rdquo;
Both &ldquo;yes&rdquo; and &ldquo;no&rdquo; appear to accept the assumption that the person has cheated.
Why It&rsquo;s Fallacious The question embeds a disputed claim instead of establishing it independently.
How to Recognize It Ask:
&ldquo;What assumptions would I be accepting simply by answering this question?&rdquo;

Visualize CLAIM │ ▼ ┌──────────┐ │ CRITERIA │ │ ✓ │ └──────────┘ │ ▼ EVIDENCE PROVIDED │ ▼ ┌──────────┐ │ CRITERIA │ │ ✕ │ ← CHANGED └──────────┘ │ ▼ NEW DEMAND Definition Moving the goalposts occurs when someone changes the requirements for accepting a claim after those requirements have been satisfied.
Example &ldquo;Show me one study proving that the treatment works.&rdquo;
After being shown a study:
&ldquo;That&rsquo;s only one study. Show me ten.&rdquo;
After ten studies:
&ldquo;Now show me that every major medical organization agrees.&rdquo;
Why It&rsquo;s Fallacious The standard for accepting the claim keeps changing rather than being applied consistently.
How to Recognize It Ask:
&ldquo;Was this new requirement part of the original standard of evidence?&rdquo;
If not, the standard may have been changed after the evidence was presented.

Definition The No True Scotsman fallacy occurs when a general claim is protected from a counterexample by redefining the relevant category to exclude the counterexample.
Example &ldquo;No true fan would criticize the team.&rdquo;
&ldquo;But Alex is a fan and he criticized the team.&rdquo;
&ldquo;Then Alex isn&rsquo;t a true fan.&rdquo;
The definition of &ldquo;fan&rdquo; has been changed specifically to protect the original claim.
Why It&rsquo;s Fallacious The argument introduces an unsupported requirement for membership only after encountering a counterexample.
How to Recognize It Ask:
&ldquo;Was this definition or requirement established before the counterexample appeared?&rdquo;

Definition Shifting the burden of proof occurs when someone making a claim attempts to make other people responsible for disproving it.
Example &ldquo;I say this device can read minds. Prove that it can&rsquo;t.&rdquo;
The person making the claim is responsible for providing evidence that the device can read minds.
Why It&rsquo;s Fallacious A claim does not gain credibility merely because others cannot disprove it.
How to Recognize It Ask:
&ldquo;Who made the claim, and who is being asked to provide the evidence?&rdquo;

Definition Special pleading occurs when someone applies a rule or standard to other cases but invents an unsupported exception for their preferred case.
Example &ldquo;Everyone should provide evidence for their claims, but my claim is based on personal experience, so I don&rsquo;t need evidence.&rdquo;
The standard is being applied selectively.
Why It&rsquo;s Fallacious A consistent standard should be applied to comparable cases unless there is a relevant reason for treating them differently.
How to Recognize It Ask:
&ldquo;Would I accept this exception if the same argument were made by someone else?&rdquo;

Definition Stereotyping occurs when a generalized belief about a group is treated as though it necessarily applies to every individual member of that group.
Example &ldquo;He&rsquo;s a teenager, so he must be irresponsible.&rdquo;
The person&rsquo;s membership in a demographic group does not establish that particular characteristic.
Why It&rsquo;s Fallacious Groups can have statistical characteristics without every individual member sharing those characteristics.
How to Recognize It Ask:
&ldquo;What evidence shows that this particular individual has the claimed characteristic?&rdquo;

Definition Whataboutism responds to an accusation or criticism by raising a different accusation against someone else.
Example &ldquo;Why did your organization violate the policy?&rdquo;
&ldquo;What about the other organization? They violated an even worse policy.&rdquo;
The second organization&rsquo;s behavior does not answer the original question.
Why It&rsquo;s Fallacious Another person&rsquo;s wrongdoing may be relevant to a broader discussion, but it does not automatically excuse or refute the original criticism.
How to Recognize It Ask:
&ldquo;Does the response address the original criticism, or does it introduce a different accusation?&rdquo;
